summary.body:
Look at the chair you are sitting on. It has legs, screws, a frame, a seat, a cushion. It exists only if those parts exist and are arranged in the right way. Pile them in a heap and there is no chair. So a **composite** — anything made of parts — depends on its parts. This is not primarily a point about assembly over time. Your body’s organs were never manufactured separately and then bolted together; they developed together in the womb. And even a chair that had always existed would still depend, at every moment, on its parts existing and being arranged as they are.

So there are two dependencies, and the second is the one that matters: at any given instant, a composite thing exists only because its parts exist and are combined then. And the whole cannot be what combines them, because the whole's existence already presupposes that combining — that would be an explanatory circle. So there must be something distinct from the composite that accounts for its parts being united.

Here is where the word gets technical. Something is **simple** in the philosophical sense when it has **no parts whatsoever** — not that it is easy to understand. Indeed, divine simplicity is one of the hardest doctrines in the book.

And “parts” is meant more broadly than beginners expect. Besides **physical parts** (legs, atoms), the tradition counts **metaphysical parts** — the distinguishable principles within a thing that are not themselves separable objects:

- **form and matter** in any physical substance;
- **essence and existence** in anything contingent;
- **substance and accidents** — the thing itself and the features it has;
- **actuality and potentiality** in anything changeable;
- **genus and specific difference** — being an animal, plus being rational.

So when chapter 2 argues that the ultimate cause of composite things must be absolutely simple, it is claiming something very strong: no physical parts, no matter, no accidents distinct from its substance, no potentiality distinct from its actuality, no essence distinct from its existence. That is why Feser can say, in chapter 6, that the five proofs converge — each identifies one kind of composition and eliminates it.

One consequence worth noting now, because it prevents much later confusion. If God is absolutely simple, then God’s power, knowledge, and goodness are not three separate components bolted together. They are one reality, which we grasp in several incomplete ways through several different concepts. Lesson 14, on analogy, explains how that can be coherent.

Simplicity is not a fringe view. Feser stresses that it has been insisted on across the tradition — by pagans like Aristotle and Plotinus, Jews like Maimonides, Muslims like Avicenna and Averroes, Christians like Augustine, Anselm, and Aquinas — and that it is the core of **classical theism**.
